/** \addtogroup area_ADS */
/** \defgroup theory_ADS Theory of the foundations
 \ingroup area_ADS

 \details The headers of `include/ADS` itself hold what the other areas take
 for granted: the abstract data types of chapters 3 and 4 of the book as
 concepts of C++20 (`ADT.h`), the preconditions, the postconditions and the
 invariants that the operations check at run time (`Contract.h`), the
 iterators that the containers share (`Iterators.h`), and the primitives of
 the processor in one portable form (`Portable.h`). This page collects the
 definitions behind them, i.e., what a specification of an abstract data type
 is and what its axioms say (see \ref theory_ADS_notation and
 \ref theory_ADS_adt), what a contract is and why each kind of contract is
 checked at its own level (see \ref theory_ADS_contracts), and the categories
 of the iterators with the cost of their steps (see \ref theory_ADS_iterators),
 while the arithmetic of the portable primitives closes the page (see
 \ref theory_ADS_portable). The algorithms of the iterators have their
 pseudocode on the page of `Iterators.h`, in the comment of their function.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_ADS_notation Definitions and notation

 A <em>specification</em> of an abstract data type is a triple
 \f$ ( D , F , A ) \f$ (section 3.2 of the book): \f$ D \f$ is a set of
 domains, one of which is the <em>designated</em> domain, i.e., the type being
 defined (e.g., the stacks), and the others are the auxiliary ones (e.g., the
 items and the booleans); \f$ F \f$ is a set of operations, each a function
 among the domains of \f$ D \f$; \f$ A \f$ is a set of axioms, which give the
 semantics of \f$ F \f$. The pair \f$ ( D , F ) \f$ is the <em>syntactic</em>
 specification, which a concept of C++20 states as the names of the
 operations and the types of their arguments and results, and \f$ A \f$ is the
 <em>semantic</em> one, which no compiler checks. The semantics may be given
 in two ways: (i) <em>algebraic</em>, by equations among the operations, those
 that observe or remove written on those that construct, as in
 \f$ \mathrm{top}( \mathrm{push}( S , v ) ) = v \f$; (ii)
 <em>constructive</em>, by the precondition and the postcondition of each
 operation, written in terms of a type already known (e.g., the finite
 sequences of items). The library states (i) in the comments of `ADT.h` and
 checks (ii) in the code, by the macros of `Contract.h`, and its tests check
 both on every realization.

 The operations are of four kinds: the <em>constructors</em>, which make a
 value of the type (`create()`, `insert()`, `push()`); the
 <em>modifiers</em>, which change it; the <em>observers</em>, which return a
 property of it (`empty()`, `top()`, `read()`); the <em>iterators</em>, which
 visit its items. A realization meets the requirement of
 <em>abstraction</em>, i.e., a program that uses the type does not depend on
 how it is realized, and that of <em>protection</em>, i.e., only the
 operations of the type act on its values; hence each realization is a class
 whose data are private, and one realization can replace another under the
 same concept (Liskov and Zilles, Parnas).

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ ( D , F , A ) \f$</td>
     <td>a specification: the domains, the operations and the
         axioms</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ s_0 , \ldots , s_{n-1} \f$</td>
     <td>the items of a SEQUENCE of length \f$ n \f$, \f$ s_i \f$ the item
         of index \f$ i \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathit{pos}_i \f$</td>
     <td>the position of \f$ s_i \f$, of the type <tt>Pos</tt> of the
         realization, for \f$ 0 \le i < n \f$; \f$ \mathit{pos}_{-1} \f$ and
         \f$ \mathit{pos}_n \f$ are the positions before the first item and
         after the last one, on which <tt>finished()</tt> is true</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \{ P \}\ \mathit{op}\ \{ Q \} \f$</td>
     <td>a contract: if the precondition \f$ P \f$ holds when the operation
         \f$ \mathit{op} \f$ starts, the postcondition \f$ Q \f$ holds when it
         ends</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ I \f$</td>
     <td>the invariant of a structure, a property of its representation that
         holds between any two of its operations</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ h \f$</td>
     <td>the height of a binary tree, the length of its longest path from
         the root</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ c \f$, \f$ m \f$</td>
     <td>the capacity of a circular vector, and the number of places of a
         table</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_ADS_adt Abstract data types and their axioms

 The concepts of `ADT.h` are the five abstract data types of chapters 3 and 4
 of the book, and the headers of the areas realize them, each in several
 ways; the concepts state the syntactic specification, so that a function
 template written on `Stack` takes any of the stacks of `include/ADS/stack`,
 and the axioms below are their semantics.

 <table>
 <caption>The concepts of <tt>ADT.h</tt>, their operations and the areas that
          realize them</caption>
 <tr><th>Concept</th><th>Abstract type</th><th>Operations</th>
     <th>Section of the book</th><th>Area</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Sequence</tt></td><td>SEQUENCE</td>
     <td><tt>empty()</tt>, <tt>finished()</tt>, <tt>head()</tt>,
         <tt>tail()</tt>, <tt>next()</tt>, <tt>prev()</tt>,
         <tt>insert()</tt>, <tt>remove()</tt>, <tt>read()</tt>,
         <tt>write()</tt></td><td>3.2.1</td>
     <td><tt>include/ADS/sequence</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Set</tt></td><td>SET</td>
     <td><tt>size()</tt>, <tt>contains()</tt>, <tt>insert()</tt>,
         <tt>remove()</tt></td><td>3.2.2</td>
     <td><tt>include/ADS/set</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Dictionary</tt></td><td>DICTIONARY</td>
     <td><tt>lookup()</tt>, <tt>insert()</tt>, <tt>remove()</tt></td>
     <td>3.2.3</td><td><tt>include/ADS/dictionary</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Stack</tt></td><td>STACK</td>
     <td><tt>empty()</tt>, <tt>push()</tt>, <tt>pop()</tt>,
         <tt>top()</tt></td><td>4.3.1</td>
     <td><tt>include/ADS/stack</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Queue</tt></td><td>QUEUE</td>
     <td><tt>empty()</tt>, <tt>enqueue()</tt>, <tt>dequeue()</tt>,
         <tt>top()</tt></td><td>4.3.1</td>
     <td><tt>include/ADS/queue</tt></td></tr>
 </table>

 The axioms of the STACK, with \f$ \mathrm{create} \f$ the empty stack, are
 \f[
   \begin{array}{ll}
   \mathrm{empty}( \mathrm{create} ) = \mathbf{true} , &
   \mathrm{empty}( \mathrm{push}( S , v ) ) = \mathbf{false} , \cr
   \mathrm{top}( \mathrm{push}( S , v ) ) = v , &
   \mathrm{pop}( \mathrm{push}( S , v ) ) = S ,
   \end{array} \tag{1}
 \f]
 while \f$ \mathrm{top}( \mathrm{create} ) \f$ and
 \f$ \mathrm{pop}( \mathrm{create} ) \f$ are undefined, i.e., `top()` and
 `pop()` are partial, with the precondition that the stack is not empty. Those
 of the QUEUE, for \f$ Q \f$ not empty, are
 \f[
   \mathrm{top}( \mathrm{enqueue}( Q , v ) ) = \mathrm{top}( Q ) , \qquad
   \mathrm{dequeue}( \mathrm{enqueue}( Q , v ) ) =
   \mathrm{enqueue}( \mathrm{dequeue}( Q ) , v ) , \tag{2}
 \f]
 with \f$ \mathrm{top}( \mathrm{enqueue}( \mathrm{create} , v ) ) = v \f$ and
 \f$ \mathrm{dequeue}( \mathrm{enqueue}( \mathrm{create} , v ) ) =
 \mathrm{create} \f$; the difference between (1) and (2) is the whole
 difference between the two types, since their syntactic specifications are
 the same up to the names. Those of the SET and of the DICTIONARY define the
 observer on each constructor, e.g., for the SET
 \f[
   \mathrm{contains}( x , \mathrm{insert}( y , A ) ) =
   ( x = y ) \lor \mathrm{contains}( x , A ) , \qquad
   \mathrm{contains}( x , \mathrm{remove}( y , A ) ) =
   ( x \ne y ) \land \mathrm{contains}( x , A ) , \tag{3}
 \f]
 with \f$ \mathrm{contains}( x , \mathrm{create} ) = \mathbf{false} \f$, so
 that inserting an item already there, or removing one that is not, leaves
 the set as it is (the library relaxes in this way the preconditions
 \f$ x \notin A \f$ and \f$ x \in A \f$ that some specifications state).

 <em>Lemma 1 (the normal form of the stacks).</em> By (1), every stack built
 by \f$ \mathrm{create} \f$, \f$ \mathrm{push} \f$ and \f$ \mathrm{pop} \f$,
 each \f$ \mathrm{pop} \f$ applied to a stack not empty, is equal to a term
 \f[
   \mathrm{push}( \cdots \mathrm{push}( \mathrm{push}( \mathrm{create} ,
   v_1 ) , v_2 ) \cdots , v_k ) , \quad k \ge 0 , \tag{4}
 \f]
 and the observers of a term (4) are known: it is empty if and only if
 \f$ k = 0 \f$, and its top is \f$ v_k \f$.

 Indeed, by induction on the number of operations of the term, a
 \f$ \mathrm{push} \f$ applied to a term (4) is a term (4), and a
 \f$ \mathrm{pop} \f$ applied to a term (4) with \f$ k \ge 1 \f$ is, by the
 last axiom of (1), the term (4) of \f$ v_1 , \ldots , v_{k-1} \f$. The axioms
 are therefore <em>sufficiently complete</em>: they decide the value of every
 observer on every stack, which is what makes them a specification rather
 than a list of properties (Guttag and Horning). The same holds for the queues
 by (2), the normal form being made of \f$ \mathrm{enqueue} \f$ alone, and
 for the sets by (3), the normal form being made of \f$ \mathrm{insert} \f$
 alone, with distinct items in any order.

 The SEQUENCE is specified in the constructive way, by its positions. As in
 the book, \f$ \mathrm{insert}( p , v ) \f$ with \f$ p = \mathit{pos}_i \f$
 puts \f$ v \f$ at the index \f$ i \f$, the old items from the index \f$ i \f$
 moving one place on, and returns the position of the new item, while
 \f$ \mathrm{remove}( p ) \f$ with \f$ p = \mathit{pos}_i \f$ removes the item
 of index \f$ i \f$ and returns the position of the new item of index
 \f$ i \f$; hence the axioms
 \f[
   \mathrm{read}( \mathrm{insert}( p , v ) ) = v , \qquad
   \mathrm{remove}( \mathrm{insert}( p , v ) ) = s , \qquad
   \mathrm{read}( \mathrm{write}( p , v ) ) = v , \tag{5}
 \f]
 the last with the other items unchanged, and
 \f$ \mathrm{empty}( s ) = \mathrm{finished}( \mathrm{head}( s ) ) \f$, which
 makes `empty()` redundant. What a position is depends on the realization
 (an index, the address of a cell, a cursor, see \ref theory_sequence), and so
 does the cost of each operation, while (5) holds on all of them.

 \section theory_ADS_contracts Contracts

 A <em>contract</em> of an operation is a pair of a precondition \f$ P \f$
 and a postcondition \f$ Q \f$, written \f$ \{ P \}\ \mathit{op}\ \{ Q \} \f$
 after Hoare: the caller ensures \f$ P \f$, and the operation, under
 \f$ P \f$, ensures \f$ Q \f$; a structure has also an <em>invariant</em>
 \f$ I \f$, which each operation that changes it may assume at its start and
 must restore at its end, i.e., \f$ \{ P \land I \}\ \mathit{op}\ \{ Q \land I
 \} \f$. A violated precondition is a bug of the caller, a violated
 postcondition or invariant a bug of the operation (Meyer); in either case
 `Contract.h` throws `contract_violation`, a `std::logic_error` whose message
 names the kind of contract, the condition, the function and the place in the
 source.

 Checking a contract costs time, and the costs differ by orders of magnitude:
 \f$ \mathrm{empty}() \f$ before a \f$ \mathrm{pop}() \f$ costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$,
 the order of a heap after an insertion costs \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$, and that a
 vector is sorted before a binary search costs \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ against
 the \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ of the search. A check of cost \f$ g( n ) \f$ added
 to an operation of cost \f$ t( n ) \f$ makes it cost
 \f$ O( t( n ) + g( n ) ) \f$, which is the same bound only if
 \f$ g( n ) = O( t( n ) ) \f$; an invariant of \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ checked
 after each of \f$ k \f$ operations of \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ makes them cost
 \f$ \Theta( k n ) \f$, i.e., a linear algorithm quadratic. The library
 therefore sorts its checks by their cost with respect to the operation, and
 the level `ADS_CONTRACTS` chooses which are evaluated.

 <table>
 <caption>The contracts of <tt>Contract.h</tt> and the level from which each is
          checked</caption>
 <tr><th>Macro</th><th>Contract</th><th>Its cost</th><th>Level</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ADS_PRE</tt></td><td>a precondition</td>
     <td>at most that of the operation</td><td>1 (the default, 0 with
     <tt>NDEBUG</tt>)</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ADS_POST</tt></td><td>a postcondition</td>
     <td>at most that of the operation</td><td>2</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ADS_INVARIANT</tt></td><td>the invariant of a structure</td>
     <td>usually \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td><td>3</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ADS_AUDIT</tt></td><td>a precondition or a postcondition</td>
     <td>more than the operation</td><td>3</td></tr>
 </table>

 At the level 1 the bounds of the operations hold as stated, since every
 precondition checked costs no more than its operation; at the level 3 they
 do not, and the level is meant for the tests. A condition is compiled at
 every level, so that it stays correct C++, but it is evaluated only from its
 own level; hence it must have no side effects, since a program must behave
 in the same way at every level. The macros mirror the `pre`, `post` and
 `contract_assert` of the contracts of C++26, by which they can be replaced
 when the compilers have them.

 \section theory_ADS_iterators Iterators

 An <em>iterator</em> is a position in a container that can be read and moved
 to the next one, and the standard library sorts the iterators into
 <em>categories</em> by the moves they allow in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time; an
 algorithm states the category it needs (`std::sort()` a random-access
 iterator, `std::reverse()` a bidirectional one), and the iterators of
 `Iterators.h` give the containers of the library the category that their
 representation allows, so that the range-for, the algorithms of `std` and
 of `std::ranges`, and those of Boost visit them.

 <table>
 <caption>The iterators of <tt>Iterators.h</tt> and their categories</caption>
 <tr><th>Iterator</th><th>Walks</th><th>Category</th>
     <th>Cost of a step</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>LinkedIterator</tt></td><td>cells linked by pointers</td>
     <td>forward</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>IndexIterator</tt></td><td>cells of a vector linked by
     indices</td><td>forward</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>CircularIterator</tt></td><td>a circular vector</td>
     <td>random access</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>IndirectIterator</tt></td><td>a vector of pointers, read
     through them</td><td>random access</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SlotIterator</tt></td><td>the occupied places of a table</td>
     <td>bidirectional</td><td>\f$ O( m / n ) \f$ amortized over a whole
     visit</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>KeyIterator</tt></td><td>the keys of what another iterator
     reads</td><td>that of the other, up to bidirectional</td>
     <td>that of the other</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>PathIterator</tt></td><td>a binary tree in symmetric order, by
     the path from the root</td><td>bidirectional</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized, \f$ O( h ) \f$ at most</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>WalkIterator</tt></td><td>the nodes of a tree by a policy</td>
     <td>forward</td><td>that of the policy</td></tr>
 </table>

 A circular vector of capacity \f$ c \f$ whose first item is at the place
 \f$ \mathit{head} \f$ holds its item of index \f$ i \f$, counted from the
 head, at the place
 \f[
   ( \mathit{head} + i ) \bmod c , \quad 0 \le i < n \le c , \tag{6}
 \f]
 so that `CircularIterator` keeps the index \f$ i \f$ rather than the place,
 its moves and its differences are those of the integers, and the order of
 the items is that of the queue rather than that of the places.

 A table of \f$ m \f$ places of which \f$ n \f$ are occupied is visited by
 `SlotIterator` in \f$ \Theta( m ) \f$ time, since each place is examined
 once, i.e., in \f$ O( m / n ) \f$ amortized time per item, which is
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ when the load \f$ n / m \f$ is bounded from below by a
 constant, as in the hash tables that halve their table when they get too
 empty; a single step may examine
 \f$ \Theta( m ) \f$ places.

 <em>Lemma 2 (the visit in symmetric order by the path).</em> Let
 `PathIterator` keep the path \f$ \pi \f$ from the root to the current node
 \f$ x \f$. The next node in symmetric order is the leftmost node of the right
 subtree of \f$ x \f$ if \f$ x \f$ has a right child, and otherwise the lowest
 ancestor of \f$ x \f$ whose left subtree contains \f$ x \f$; a whole visit of
 a tree of \f$ n \f$ nodes costs \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$, i.e., \f$ O( 1 ) \f$
 amortized per step, and a single step costs \f$ O( h ) \f$.

 Indeed, the nodes of the right subtree of \f$ x \f$ come right after
 \f$ x \f$, the leftmost first; if there is no right subtree, all the nodes
 below the ancestor \f$ y \f$ described come before \f$ y \f$ and \f$ x \f$
 is the last of its left subtree, so that \f$ y \f$ follows \f$ x \f$. The
 path \f$ \pi \f$ gives the ancestors without links to the parents, and a step
 that goes down pushes the nodes it meets on \f$ \pi \f$, a step that goes up
 pops them. Each node is pushed once, when the visit first reaches it from its
 parent, and popped once, when the visit leaves its subtree; hence a whole
 visit makes \f$ n \f$ pushes and \f$ n \f$ pops, and since a step moves
 along one path from the root, it makes at most \f$ h \f$ of them. The path
 takes \f$ O( h ) \f$ space, against the link to the parent in every node
 that an iterator with no path needs, i.e., \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ space; on a
 balanced tree \f$ h = O( \log n ) \f$.

 \section theory_ADS_portable Portable primitives

 The product of two words of 64 bits is a number of 128 bits, which the
 processors give in one instruction and the languages do not; `Portable.h`
 takes it from the builtin of the compiler where there is one, and otherwise
 computes it on the halves of 32 bits of the factors. With
 \f$ a = a_1 2^{32} + a_0 \f$ and \f$ b = b_1 2^{32} + b_0 \f$,
 \f$ 0 \le a_0 , a_1 , b_0 , b_1 < 2^{32} \f$, one has
 \f[
   a b = a_1 b_1 2^{64} + ( a_0 b_1 + a_1 b_0 ) 2^{32} + a_0 b_0 , \tag{7}
 \f]
 four products of 32 bits, each of which fits in 64 bits. The sum of the
 middle terms with the high half of \f$ a_0 b_0 \f$ is computed on the low
 halves of the middle products, i.e., as three numbers below \f$ 2^{32} \f$,
 whose sum is below \f$ 3 \cdot 2^{32} \f$ and fits in a word with no
 overflow; its high half is the carry into the high word of the product. In
 the same way, the signed integers of 128 bits, where the compiler has no
 `__int128`, are two words in two's complement, and the sum carries 1 into
 the high word exactly when the sum of the low words, modulo \f$ 2^{64} \f$,
 is smaller than either of them. These are details of the implementation,
 out of the API reference.

